## 5. Security boundaries (red lines #2/#3 preserved)

| Boundary | Status | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| All execution through `ai_core.execution.run` policy gate | ✅ | `capability_runtime.execute` calls only `_execution_run`; verified `kill_process`→`policy_denied` without reaching `execute_tool`. |
| `default_deny=True` preserved | ✅ | `run()` NONE path still calls `policy_engine.evaluate(..., default_deny=True)` and blocks NEVER/dangerous. |
| No second Policy/Permission/Capability Registry/EventBus/Memory | ✅ | Only new module = `capability_runtime.py`; reuses `capability_os`, `policy_engine`, `ai_core.execution`, `permission_guard`. |
| HIGH/CRITICAL blocked by Policy | ✅ | `kill_process` returns `被安全策略阻止` → `policy_denied`; executor never called. |
| Remote whitelist enforced at boundary | ✅ | `run_shell` with `allowed=["get_time"]` → `policy_denied`, executor not called. |
| No fabricated execution chains | ✅ | E2E uses real low-risk local tools (`get_time`); dangerous paths observed via monkeypatch, not faked. |

> **Self-caught regression during smoke test:** an earlier draft let registered tools call `capability_os.invoke_capability` (which bypasses the `ai_core.execution.run` policy gate). Fixed before any commit — all paths now go through `_execution_run`.

